  "slug": "crispri-responses-25da",
  "summary": "New preprints quantify how poorly CRISPRi and drug-perturbation transcriptional responses generalize across cell lines, cohorts and datasets.",
  "synthesis": "The dominant thread is quantifying how far perturbation responses generalize. CRISPRi Perturb-seq of essential genes in four human cell lines gives a median cross-line response correlation of 0.52, and transfer to a new line needs about 30% of it screened. Rejuvenation rankings from CRISPRi screens shift with the aging cohort and track cell growth programs, which suggests proliferation confounding. A comparison of six drug-perturbation resources finds high treated-expression agreement but limited concordance of drug-induced responses across sources. Related methods papers address technical confounding and prediction: cross-modal batch correction, DeepRNAi for siRNA on- and off-target activity, and ARC-Impute for spatial gene imputation. These are adjacent rather than CRISPRi-specific.",
  "synthesis_model": "claude-sonnet-5-5",
  "synthesized_on": "2026-11-20"
}
